            <div class="course-header">📘 DU &amp; LPA – A to Z</div>

            <!-- 1. Foundations -->
            <section id="du-1" class="card">
                <h2>1. Foundations of Automated Underwriting <span class="badge">DU &amp; LPA</span></h2>

                <h3>What are DU and LPA?</h3>
                <div class="table-wrap">
                    <table>
                        <thead>
                            <tr><th>System</th><th>Full Name</th><th>Owner</th><th>Used By</th></tr>
                        </thead>
                        <tbody>
                            <tr><td><strong>DU</strong></td><td>Desktop Underwriter</td><td>Fannie Mae</td><td>Lenders (final underwriting)</td></tr>
                            <tr><td><strong>DO</strong></td><td>Desktop Originator</td><td>Fannie Mae</td><td>Brokers / TPOs (origination)</td></tr>
                            <tr><td><strong>LPA</strong></td><td>Loan Product Advisor</td><td>Freddie Mac</td><td>Lenders</td></tr>
                        </tbody>
                    </table>
                </div>

                <div class="note">
                    <strong>🔑 Both are tools, not replacements for human underwriters.</strong> Neither evaluates compliance with federal/state laws, nor analyzes information outside program scope.
                </div>

                <h3>Scopes of Analysis</h3>
                <div class="grid-2">
                    <div class="tile">
                        <h5>💳 Credit Risk Factors</h5>
                        <ul>
                            <li>Credit history &amp; delinquencies</li>
                            <li>Installment &amp; mortgage accounts</li>
                            <li>Foreclosures, bankruptcies</li>
                            <li>Revolving credit utilization</li>
                            <li>Collections, inquiries</li>
                        </ul>
                    </div>
                    <div class="tile">
                        <h5>🏠 Non-Credit Risk Factors</h5>
                        <ul>
                            <li>LTV, CLTV, HLTV</li>
                            <li>Liquid reserves</li>
                            <li>Loan purpose, terms, amortization</li>
                            <li>Occupancy, ratios, property type</li>
                            <li>Co-borrowers, self-employment</li>
                        </ul>
                    </div>
                </div>

                <h3>Loan Process Flow</h3>
                <div class="process-step"><span class="num">1</span><span class="desc"><strong>Application</strong> – borrower/originator enters data into AUS (directly or via LOS)</span></div>
                <div class="process-step"><span class="num">2</span><span class="desc"><strong>Credit Report Ordered</strong> – typed to loan's AUS file</span></div>
                <div class="process-step"><span class="num">3</span><span class="desc"><strong>Underwriting Requested</strong> – originator sees if borrower qualifies</span></div>
                <div class="process-step"><span class="num">4</span><span class="desc"><strong>Processing</strong> – gather reports, request missing docs, order verifications &amp; appraisal</span></div>
                <div class="process-step"><span class="num">5</span><span class="desc"><strong>AUS Update</strong> – processor updates AUS and sends to underwriter</span></div>
                <div class="process-step"><span class="num">6</span><span class="desc"><strong>Underwriter Review</strong> – compare file to findings, validate income, ensure data accuracy</span></div>
                <div class="process-step"><span class="num">7</span><span class="desc"><strong>Final AUS Run</strong> – confirm approve/accept eligible &amp; messaging complied with</span></div>
                <div class="process-step"><span class="num">8</span><span class="desc"><strong>Closing</strong> – when final conditions received and updated findings are in</span></div>

                <h3>Reps &amp; Warrants</h3>
                <p>A set of assurances lenders make to the GSE regarding loan quality. Relief is provided when:</p>
                <ul>
                    <li>Information entered is validated to be accurate</li>
                    <li>All messaging and agency guidelines are complied with</li>
                    <li>Final recommendation matches all final documents</li>
                    <li>Appropriate Special Feature Code (SFC 127 for DU) is used</li>
                </ul>
                <div class="note danger">
                    <strong>Relief voided</strong> if lender fails to validate employment, misses VOE requirements, or misrepresents data. Lender must buy back the loan.
                </div>
            </section>

            <!-- 2. Credit Reports & Data -->
            <section id="du-2" class="card">
                <h2>2. Credit Reports &amp; Data Integrity</h2>

                <h3>Credit Report Options</h3>
                <div class="grid-3">
                    <div class="tile"><h5>Credit Only</h5><p>Order credit report without underwriting analysis.</p></div>
                    <div class="tile"><h5>Credit &amp; Underwriting</h5><p>Order credit and run underwriting.</p></div>
                    <div class="tile"><h5>Underwriting Only</h5><p>Rerun underwriting with existing credit report.</p></div>
                </div>

                <h3>Joint vs. Individual Credit</h3>
                <div class="table-wrap">
                    <table>
                        <thead><tr><th>Scenario</th><th>Application</th><th>Credit Report</th></tr></thead>
                        <tbody>
                            <tr><td>Married / co-mingled</td><td>Joint</td><td>Joint</td></tr>
                            <tr><td>Non-related borrowers</td><td>Individual</td><td>Individual</td></tr>
                            <tr><td>Unmarried co-habitating (commingled)</td><td>Joint</td><td>Joint</td></tr>
                            <tr><td>Unmarried co-habitating (not commingled)</td><td>Individual</td><td>Individual</td></tr>
                            <tr><td>Multiple investors (3+)</td><td>Individual</td><td>Individual</td></tr>
                        </tbody>
                    </table>
                </div>
                <div class="note warning">
                    <strong>Maximum 4 borrowers</strong> supported by DU/LPA. 5+ requires manual underwriting (only if lender/investor allows).
                </div>

                <h3>Data Integrity — "Garbage in, garbage out"</h3>
                <p>Critical fields to verify:</p>
                <ul>
                    <li>Borrower name / DOB / SSN</li>
                    <li>Loan terms (update when term changes)</li>
                    <li>LTV / CLTV / HLTV (include subordinate financing)</li>
                    <li>Sales price, appraised value</li>
                    <li>Income amounts &amp; sources</li>
                    <li>DTI ratios</li>
                    <li>Asset amounts &amp; sources</li>
                    <li>Property / occupancy type</li>
                </ul>
            </section>

            <!-- 3. DU Red Flags -->
            <section id="du-3" class="card">
                <h2>3. DU Red Flags &amp; Tolerances</h2>

                <h3>Red Flag Messages</h3>
                <div class="grid-2">
                    <div class="tile">
                        <h5>🚩 Excessive Resubmissions</h5>
                        <p>50-60 runs indicates possible manipulation. After 19 runs, flag stays permanently. <strong>Action:</strong> Verify all data validated and messaging documented.</p>
                    </div>
                    <div class="tile">
                        <h5>🚩 Frozen Credit Report</h5>
                        <p>One or more bureaus frozen. If one frozen, proceed if data from 2 repositories available. If two frozen, borrower must remove freeze.</p>
                    </div>
                    <div class="tile">
                        <h5>🚩 Potential Case File ID Reuse</h5>
                        <p>Critical data changed (e.g., owner-occupant → investor). Investigate; open new DU if second loan.</p>
                    </div>
                    <div class="tile">
                        <h5>🚩 Occupancy Modified</h5>
                        <p>Original DU run as investment, changed to owner-occupied. Could be misrepresentation — investigate with due diligence.</p>
                    </div>
                </div>

                <h3>DU Recommendations</h3>
                <div class="table-wrap">
                    <table>
                        <thead><tr><th>Recommendation</th><th>Meaning</th></tr></thead>
                        <tbody>
                            <tr><td><strong>Approve Eligible</strong></td><td>Meets credit risk AND loan eligibility standards</td></tr>
                            <tr><td><strong>Approve Ineligible</strong></td><td>Meets credit risk but not loan guidelines</td></tr>
                            <tr><td><strong>Refer with Caution</strong></td><td>Does not meet credit risk or eligibility; ineligible for delivery</td></tr>
                            <tr><td><strong>Out of Scope</strong></td><td>DU unable to underwrite as submitted</td></tr>
                        </tbody>
                    </table>
                </div>

                <h3>DTI Tolerances</h3>
                <div class="table-wrap">
                    <table>
                        <thead><tr><th>Original → New</th><th>Resubmit?</th><th>Reason</th></tr></thead>
                        <tbody>
                            <tr><td>35% → 40%</td><td>✅ Yes</td><td>Exceeds 3% tolerance</td></tr>
                            <tr><td>44% → 46%</td><td>✅ Yes</td><td>Goes over 45%</td></tr>
                            <tr><td>46% → 48%</td><td>❌ No</td><td>Only 2% increase; already &gt;45%</td></tr>
                            <tr><td>48% → 51%</td><td>✅ Yes</td><td>Exceeds 50% max</td></tr>
                        </tbody>
                    </table>
                </div>

                <h3>Other Tolerances</h3>
                <ul>
                    <li><strong>Rate increase:</strong> Always resubmit</li>
                    <li><strong>Rate decrease (not from buyown):</strong> No resubmit</li>
                    <li><strong>Funds to close exceed findings:</strong> Document sufficient assets; no resubmit</li>
                    <li><strong>Reserves less than required:</strong> Verify ≥90% of required; no resubmit</li>
                    <li><strong>Refinance loan amount:</strong> May increase $500 or 1% (whichever less); may decrease 5%</li>
                    <li><strong>LLPA differences:</strong> Resubmit when changes occur</li>
                </ul>
            </section>

            <!-- 4. LPA Guidelines -->
            <section id="du-4" class="card">
                <h2>4. LPA Specific Guidelines</h2>

                <h3>LPA Recommendations</h3>
                <div class="table-wrap">
                    <table>
                        <thead><tr><th>Recommendation</th><th>Meaning</th></tr></thead>
                        <tbody>
                            <tr><td><strong>Accept</strong></td><td>Meets Freddie Mac credit risk standards</td></tr>
                            <tr><td><strong>Caution</strong></td><td>Unlikely to meet standards; excessive layered risk</td></tr>
                            <tr><td><strong>Streamline Accept</strong></td><td>Allows reduced documentation</td></tr>
                            <tr><td><strong>Standard Documentation</strong></td><td>Most comprehensive; required for higher risk</td></tr>
                            <tr><td><strong>Ineligible/Incomplete/Invalid</strong></td><td>Unable to underwrite as submitted</td></tr>
                        </tbody>
                    </table>
                </div>

                <h3>LPA Tolerances (No Resubmission)</h3>
                <ul>
                    <li>Monthly debt payment decreases</li>
                    <li>Income for any borrower increases</li>
                    <li>Income decreases if DTI doesn't increase &gt;3% or go over 45%</li>
                    <li>Debt increases if DTI doesn't increase &gt;3% or go over 45%</li>
                    <li>Verified assets/reserves increase</li>
                    <li>Verified reserves decrease by no less than amount required</li>
                    <li>Loan amount decreases ≤5% on refinance (if MI not impacted)</li>
                </ul>
            </section>

            <!-- 5. Total Scorecard -->
            <section id="du-5" class="card">
                <h2>5. Total Scorecard (FHA/VA)</h2>
                <p><strong>TOTAL</strong> = Technology Open to Approved Lenders. An overlay accessed through DU and LPA — <em>not</em> a standalone AUS.</p>

                <div class="table-wrap">
                    <table>
                        <thead><tr><th>Recommendation</th><th>Meaning</th></tr></thead>
                        <tbody>
                            <tr><td><strong>Accept Eligible</strong></td><td>May be eligible for insurance; docs must be validated</td></tr>
                            <tr><td><strong>Refer</strong></td><td>Must be underwritten by a DE underwriter; may require manual underwrite</td></tr>
                            <tr><td><strong>Ineligible</strong></td><td>FICO/credit may be eligible but other characteristics ineligible</td></tr>
                        </tbody>
                    </table>
                </div>

                <div class="note">
                    <strong>FHA:</strong> Accept eligible means FHA will insure without manual underwriting unless manual downgrade needed. Cannot accept/deny based solely on Total Scorecard.
                </div>
            </section>

            <!-- 6. Lender Responsibilities -->
            <section id="du-6" class="card">
                <h2>6. Lender Responsibilities</h2>
                <ul>
                    <li><strong>Confirm accuracy of data</strong> – file must match AUS exactly</li>
                    <li><strong>Employ prudent underwriting judgment</strong> – don't put loan under microscope, but don't give away the farm</li>
                    <li><strong>Comply with all verification messages</strong> – read findings carefully</li>
                    <li><strong>Exercise due diligence</strong> – research unidentified deductions, large recurring payments</li>
                    <li><strong>Ensure derogatory/contradictory info is part of findings</strong> – e.g., self-employment contradiction, tax lien in declarations</li>
                    <li><strong>Take action when errors not referenced</strong> – rerun AUS with corrected data</li>
                    <li><strong>Review credit reports</strong> – trade lines current? 30-day accounts marked correctly? Student loans reflected?</li>
                    <li><strong>Update LOS and AUS with every change</strong></li>
                </ul>
            </section>

            <!-- 7. Non-Traditional Credit -->
            <section id="du-7" class="card">
                <h2>7. Underwriting Without Traditional Credit</h2>

                <div class="grid-2">
                    <div class="tile">
                        <h5>DU Requirements (No Score)</h5>
                        <ul>
                            <li>Primary residence, all occupy</li>
                            <li>One unit, purchase or limited cash-out refi</li>
                            <li>Fixed-rate, conforming limits (not high-balance)</li>
                            <li>Max LTV/CLTV/HLTV: 90%</li>
                            <li>DTI &lt; 40%</li>
                        </ul>
                        <p><strong>All borrowers no score:</strong> No non-traditional credit verification required; DU cash flow assessment; 12-month asset verification may strengthen.</p>
                        <p><strong>At least one has score:</strong> Document non-traditional credit only if ≥50% qualifying income from borrowers without scores.</p>
                    </div>
                    <div class="tile">
                        <h5>LPA Requirements (No Score)</h5>
                        <ul>
                            <li>Purchase or no cash-out refi</li>
                            <li>One-unit, all occupy as primary</li>
                            <li>Fixed-rate</li>
                            <li>Max LTV/TLTV/HTLTV: 95%</li>
                            <li>Standard conforming limits (no super conforming)</li>
                            <li>No collections (other than medical), no judgments/tax liens in 24 months</li>
                        </ul>
                        <p>If all borrowers use only non-credit references, at least one must participate in homeownership program.</p>
                    </div>
                </div>

                <div class="note">
                    <strong>Document two non-traditional credit sources for each borrower</strong> – each with 12-month pay history; one must be housing-related (e.g., rent).
                </div>
            </section>

            <!-- 8. Validation Services -->
            <section id="du-8" class="card">
                <h2>8. Validation Services (D1C &amp; AIM)</h2>

                <div class="grid-2">
                    <div class="tile">
                        <h5>Fannie Mae: Day One Certainty (D1C)</h5>
                        <p>Uses third-party vendor reports for VOE, VOI, VOA. If validated, no further documentation required. Provides relief from reps and warrants.</p>
                    </div>
                    <div class="tile">
                        <h5>Freddie Mac: Asset &amp; Income Modeler (AIM)</h5>
                        <p>Same concept — integrates electronic data from approved vendors. Validation messages: Eligible / Not Eligible / Unavailable.</p>
                    </div>
                </div>

                <h3>What Can Be Validated</h3>
                <ul>
                    <li>Employment (via The Work Number, etc.)</li>
                    <li>Income (bonus, OT, commission — ~80% of types)</li>
                    <li>Assets (60 days transaction history)</li>
                    <li>Tax transcripts (self-employment, retirement, Social Security)</li>
                </ul>

                <h3>What Cannot Be Validated</h3>
                <ul>
                    <li>Military service members</li>
                    <li>Child support or alimony</li>
                    <li>Self-employed employment (income may be validated)</li>
                    <li>Self-employed income on Form 1065 or 1120S</li>
                    <li>Gifts or business assets for self-employed borrower</li>
                </ul>

                <div class="note">
                    <strong>Documentation expiration:</strong> Same as any other — not over 120 days old at note execution. Exception: VOE must be updated within 10 calendar days of closing.
                </div>

                <h3>Benefits</h3>
                <ul>
                    <li>Up to 8 days saved if only assets validated</li>
                    <li>Up to 12 days saved when employment &amp; income validated</li>
                    <li>Up to 15 days faster with appraisal waiver</li>
                    <li>Relief from reps and warrants</li>
                </ul>
            </section>

            <!-- 9. UCDP -->
            <section id="du-9" class="card">
                <h2>9. Uniform Collateral Data Portal (UCDP)</h2>
                <p>Electronic delivery system for appraisals to Fannie Mae and Freddie Mac. <strong>For lenders only</strong> — AMCs submit as agent.</p>

                <h3>Key Facts</h3>
                <ul>
                    <li>Conventional loans requiring appraisal (not government)</li>
                    <li>All appraisals must be uploaded: single family, manufactured, 2-4 units, condos, co-ops, exterior-only, repair inspections (1004D/442), revisions</li>
                    <li>Maintains up to <strong>3 appraisals</strong> per loan</li>
                    <li>Field reviews are <em>not</em> submitted</li>
                    <li>Up to 10 appraisals at a time</li>
                    <li>Must receive <strong>Successful</strong> SSR status before delivery</li>
                </ul>

                <h3>SSR Message Types</h3>
                <div class="grid-3">
                    <div class="tile"><h5>🔴 Hard Stop</h5><p>Must be cleared</p></div>
                    <div class="tile"><h5>🟡 Warning / Overridable</h5><p>Manually overwrite or upload corrected appraisal</p></div>
                    <div class="tile"><h5>⚫ Fatal</h5><p>Missing data; obtain corrected appraisal and resubmit</p></div>
                </div>
            </section>

            <!-- 10. Collateral Underwriter -->
            <section id="du-10" class="card">
                <h2>10. Collateral Underwriter (CU) &amp; ACE</h2>

                <div class="grid-2">
                    <div class="tile">
                        <h5>Fannie Mae: Collateral Underwriter</h5>
                        <ul>
                            <li>Risk score 1.0 (low) to 5.0 (high)</li>
                            <li>Available on one-unit attached/detached condos (not manufactured)</li>
                            <li><strong>Risk score ≤2.5</strong> for relief from standard collateral reps and warrants</li>
                            <li>Still need Form 442 on required repairs</li>
                        </ul>
                    </div>
                    <div class="tile">
                        <h5>Freddie Mac: ACE</h5>
                        <ul>
                            <li>LPA must provide <strong>Accept</strong> risk class</li>
                            <li>Single-family one-unit (incl. condos)</li>
                            <li>Primary or second home</li>
                            <li>Purchase/No cash-out: Max LTV ≤90%</li>
                            <li>Cash-out (primary): ≤70%; (second): ≤60%</li>
                        </ul>
                    </div>
                </div>

                <h3>When Appraisal Waiver Cannot Be Used (DU)</h3>
                <ul>
                    <li>Lender has knowledge appraisal is warranted</li>
                    <li>Proposed construction / construction-to-perm</li>
                    <li>2-4 units when value ≥$1 million</li>
                    <li>HomeStyle, Texas Section 50(a)(6)</li>
                    <li>Leaseholds, resale restrictions, co-ops, manufactured homes</li>
                    <li>Gift of equity</li>
                    <li>Major repairs required affecting safety/soundness</li>
                    <li>Borrower requests appraisal</li>
                </ul>

                <div class="note warning">
                    <strong>Due diligence with waiver:</strong> FEMA disaster area — verify no damage. Home inspection — confirm not C6. Refinance — certify not listed for sale (cash-out not acquired in last 6 months).
                </div>
            </section>

            <!-- 11. DU Quiz -->
            <section id="du-11" class="card">
                <h2>11. DU &amp; LPA Practice Quiz</h2>

                <div class="quiz-q"><p>1. Which is NOT a function of DU or LPA?</p><div class="quiz-answer"><strong>D.</strong> Verify compliance with all state and federal laws and regulations.</div></div>
                <div class="quiz-q"><p>2. An AUS analyzes what type of risk factors?</p><div class="quiz-answer"><strong>Credit and non-credit risk factors.</strong></div></div>
                <div class="quiz-q"><p>3. True or False: Lender must ensure final AUS matches file documentation.</p><div class="quiz-answer"><strong>True.</strong></div></div>
                <div class="quiz-q"><p>4. AUS documentation requirements are found in which section?</p><div class="quiz-answer"><strong>Message condition section.</strong></div></div>
                <div class="quiz-q"><p>5. Inaccurate data entry can result in what kind of loan?</p><div class="quiz-answer"><strong>All of the above</strong> – invalid, undeliverable, unsellable.</div></div>
                <div class="quiz-q"><p>6. When receiving a DU red flag for excessive submissions, you must:</p><div class="quiz-answer"><strong>C.</strong> Review the loan file for continued accuracy and integrity.</div></div>
                <div class="quiz-q"><p>7. True or False: Total Scorecard is a standalone underwriting program.</p><div class="quiz-answer"><strong>False.</strong> Must be used with DU/LPA.</div></div>
                <div class="quiz-q"><p>8. Data the AUS may not assess includes:</p><div class="quiz-answer"><strong>Credit report inaccuracies.</strong></div></div>
                <div class="quiz-q"><p>9. Incorrect borrower SSN may result in a DU:</p><div class="quiz-answer"><strong>Error code.</strong></div></div>
                <div class="quiz-q"><p>10. Even with approve/accept, underwriter must always employ:</p><div class="quiz-answer"><strong>Prudent underwriting judgment.</strong></div></div>
            </section>

            <!-- ==================== TRID COURSE ==================== -->
            <div class="course-header" style="margin-top:1rem;">📗 TRID Compliance</div>

            <!-- 1. Purpose -->
            <section id="trid-1" class="card">
                <h2>1. Purpose of TRID <span class="badge green">Know Before You Owe</span></h2>
                <p>TRID (TILA‑RESPA Integrated Disclosure) is a CFPB tool to ensure lenders follow the Dodd‑Frank <span class="highlight">"Know Before You Owe"</span> rule. It protects and informs consumers by simplifying the loan process and reducing paperwork.</p>

                <div class="note">
                    <strong>🔑 Applies to closed‑end loans only.</strong> Not reverse mortgages, HELOCs, mobile homes without land, or certain temporary/construction loans.
                </div>

                <h3>Before vs. After</h3>
                <div class="grid-2">
                    <div class="tile">
                        <h5>📄 Before (4 documents)</h5>
                        <ul>
                            <li>Good Faith Estimate (GFE)</li>
                            <li>Initial Truth in Lending (TIL)</li>
                            <li>HUD‑1 Settlement Statement</li>
                            <li>Final TIL</li>
                        </ul>
                    </div>
                    <div class="tile">
                        <h5>📄 After (2 documents)</h5>
                        <ul>
                            <li><strong>Loan Estimate (LE)</strong> – GFE + initial TIL</li>
                            <li><strong>Closing Disclosure (CD)</strong> – HUD‑1 + final TIL</li>
                        </ul>
                    </div>
                </div>

                <h3>Application Trigger</h3>
                <p>The loan application is triggered when <strong>six pieces</strong> are collected. LE must be delivered within <strong>3 business days</strong>.</p>
                <div class="process-step"><span class="num">1</span><span class="desc">Full name</span></div>
                <div class="process-step"><span class="num">2</span><span class="desc">Social Security Number</span></div>
                <div class="process-step"><span class="num">3</span><span class="desc">Income</span></div>
                <div class="process-step"><span class="num">4</span><span class="desc">Property address</span></div>
                <div class="process-step"><span class="num">5</span><span class="desc">Estimated value</span></div>
                <div class="process-step"><span class="num">6</span><span class="desc">Desired loan amount</span></div>
            </section>

            <!-- 2. Forms -->
            <section id="trid-2" class="card">
                <h2>2. New Forms &amp; Acronyms</h2>
                <div class="grid-3">
                    <div class="tile"><h5>📌 LE</h5><p>Loan Estimate — within 3 business days of application. Shows terms, costs, cash to close.</p></div>
                    <div class="tile"><h5>📌 CD</h5><p>Closing Disclosure — received 3 business days before consummation. Final terms and costs.</p></div>
                    <div class="tile"><h5>📌 Acronyms</h5><p>APR, TILA, RESPA, CFPB, LE, CD, TRID, NMLS, MLO</p></div>
                </div>

                <h3>New Data Points</h3>
                <ul>
                    <li>Realtor's license number</li>
                    <li>Services borrower can/cannot shop for</li>
                    <li>Payment comparison over loan term</li>
                    <li>Lender's responsibility for appraisal copy</li>
                    <li>Rate lock confirmation</li>
                    <li>Contact info + NMLS IDs</li>
                </ul>

                <div class="note warning">
                    <strong>🚫 Pre‑disclosure restriction:</strong> You cannot create a "fake" LE‑looking form for pre‑qualification.
                </div>
            </section>

            <!-- 3. Loan Estimate -->
            <section id="trid-3" class="card">
                <h2>3. Loan Estimate (LE)</h2>
                <p>Delivered <strong>within 3 business days</strong> of application. Shows accurate terms and costs.</p>

                <div class="table-wrap">
                    <table>
                        <thead><tr><th>Section</th><th>Includes</th></tr></thead>
                        <tbody>
                            <tr><td><strong>Loan Terms</strong></td><td>Amount, rate, monthly P&amp;I, prepayment penalty, MI &amp; payment changes</td></tr>
                            <tr><td><strong>Closing Cost Details</strong></td><td>
                                <ul>
                                    <li>A. Origination Charges</li>
                                    <li>B. Services You Cannot Shop For</li>
                                    <li>C. Services You Can Shop For</li>
                                    <li>E. Taxes &amp; Other Government Fees</li>
                                    <li>F. Prepaids</li>
                                    <li>G. Initial Escrow Payment</li>
                                </ul>
                            </td></tr>
                            <tr><td><strong>Cash to Close</strong></td><td>Down payment, deposits, seller credits, adjustments</td></tr>
                            <tr><td><strong>Other Considerations</strong></td><td>Appraisal, assumption, insurance, late payment, refinance, servicing</td></tr>
                            <tr><td><strong>Confirm Receipt</strong></td><td>Signature line – proof of receipt</td></tr>
                        </tbody>
                    </table>
                </div>

                <div class="note">
                    <strong>📬 Delivery:</strong> If not electronic (with acknowledgment), must be mailed within 3 business days. Business day = day creditor is open for substantially all business (Sat. usually excluded).
                </div>
                <div class="note warning">
                    <strong>⏳ Intent to Proceed:</strong> Required before collecting any fees (except credit report).
                </div>
            </section>

            <!-- 4. Closing Disclosure -->
            <section id="trid-4" class="card">
                <h2>4. Closing Disclosure (CD)</h2>
                <p>Must be <strong>received by borrower at least 3 business days before consummation</strong>. Similar to LE but final figures and more data.</p>

                <h3>Key Additions</h3>
                <ul>
                    <li>Settlement agent, file number, seller info, MIC number</li>
                    <li>LE vs. final cost comparison (line‑item)</li>
                    <li>"Paid by others" column (including lender credits)</li>
                    <li>Recording fees lumped together</li>
                    <li>Owner's title policy disclosure</li>
                    <li>APR, finance charge, TIP</li>
                    <li>Escrow account details / waiver</li>
                    <li>NMLS ID placement (must be on correct line)</li>
                </ul>

                <div class="note">
                    <strong>✍️ Delivery:</strong> Electronic or mail. If mail, place in mail <strong>no later than 3 business days</strong> before consummation. Business day for CD = all calendar days except Sundays and legal holidays (Saturdays count).
                </div>
                <div class="note warning">
                    <strong>⏰ Redisclosure wait:</strong> New 3‑business‑day waiting period if APR changes beyond tolerance, loan product changes, or prepayment penalty added.
                </div>

                <h3>Who Provides the CD?</h3>
                <ul>
                    <li><strong>Settlement agent</strong> must provide to seller.</li>
                    <li>Lender can contract settlement agent to provide to buyer — usually lender retains responsibility.</li>
                    <li>Cash transactions: CD not required, but state laws may require a form.</li>
                </ul>
            </section>

            <!-- 5. Timing -->
            <section id="trid-5" class="card">
                <h2>5. Timing Is Everything ⏱️</h2>
                <div class="grid-2">
                    <div class="tile">
                        <h5>📅 Loan Estimate</h5>
                        <ul>
                            <li>Within <strong>3 business days</strong> of application</li>
                            <li>Business day = creditor open for substantially all business (Sat. excluded)</li>
                            <li>If electronic not acknowledged by day 3, must mail</li>
                            <li>Intent to Proceed required before fees (except credit report)</li>
                        </ul>
                    </div>
                    <div class="tile">
                        <h5>📅 Closing Disclosure</h5>
                        <ul>
                            <li>Received at least <strong>3 business days</strong> before consummation</li>
                            <li>Business day = all calendar days except Sundays &amp; legal holidays (Sat. counts)</li>
                            <li>Mail: place in mail no later than 3 business days before closing</li>
                            <li>Redisclosure triggers new 3‑day waiting period</li>
                        </ul>
                    </div>
                </div>

                <div class="note">
                    <strong>📬 Electronic delivery:</strong> Allowed, but must follow E‑SIGN and privacy rules. Acknowledgment of receipt is critical.
                </div>
            </section>

            <!-- 6. Fee Tolerances -->
            <section id="trid-6" class="card">
                <h2>6. Fee Tolerance Limits</h2>

                <div class="grid-3">
                    <div class="tile">
                        <h5>🔴 Zero Tolerance</h5>
                        <p><strong>Cannot increase</strong> unless valid change of circumstance.</p>
                        <ul>
                            <li>Lender/broker fees</li>
                            <li>Transfer taxes</li>
                            <li>FHA/VA upfront premiums (if disclosed incorrectly)</li>
                            <li>Services borrower cannot shop for</li>
                        </ul>
                    </div>
                    <div class="tile">
                        <h5>🟡 10% Cumulative</h5>
                        <p><strong>Total cannot increase more than 10%.</strong></p>
                        <ul>
                            <li>Recording fees</li>
                            <li>Services borrower can shop for (from lender's list)</li>
                        </ul>
                        <p>Sum all fees; if total &gt;10%, lender pays difference.</p>
                    </div>
                    <div class="tile">
                        <h5>🟢 No Limit / Good Faith</h5>
                        <p><strong>Can increase freely</strong> if good faith and changed circumstances.</p>
                        <ul>
                            <li>Prepaids (insurance, interest)</li>
                            <li>Escrow deposits</li>
                            <li>Property taxes</li>
                        </ul>
                    </div>
                </div>

                <div class="note danger">
                    <strong>🚨 Human error is not a change of circumstance.</strong> Underestimating FHA premiums, transfer taxes, or lender fees — lender absorbs the cost.
                </div>
            </section>

            <!-- 7. Redisclosing -->
            <section id="trid-7" class="card">
                <h2>7. Redisclosing &amp; Change of Circumstance</h2>
                <p>A <strong>valid change of circumstance</strong> allows redisclosure of the LE (and possibly new CD waiting period). Must be:</p>
                <ul>
                    <li>Unexpected event beyond anyone's control</li>
                    <li>New information not previously available</li>
                    <li>Documented in the loan file</li>
                </ul>

                <div class="grid-2">
                    <div class="tile">
                        <h5>✅ Valid</h5>
                        <ul>
                            <li>Rate lock (points/fees change)</li>
                            <li>Bona fide discount point buy‑down</li>
                            <li>Appraisal different than expected</li>
                            <li>Title issues (e.g., quitclaim deed)</li>
                            <li>Natural disaster, job loss</li>
                            <li>Loan product change (documented)</li>
                        </ul>
                    </div>
                    <div class="tile">
                        <h5>❌ Not Valid</h5>
                        <ul>
                            <li>Human error / underestimating costs</li>
                            <li>Adding forgotten lender fees</li>
                            <li>Changing origination fees without valid reason</li>
                        </ul>
                    </div>
                </div>

                <div class="note">
                    <strong>📝 Document everything:</strong> Keep letters, system logs, and explanations for any redisclosure. Auditors will ask for proof.
                </div>
            </section>

            <!-- 8. TRID Quiz -->
            <section id="trid-8" class="card">
                <h2>8. TRID Practice Quiz</h2>

                <div class="quiz-q"><p>1. True or False: CFPB requires TRID for open‑ended loans.</p><div class="quiz-answer"><strong>False.</strong> Closed‑end loans only.</div></div>
                <div class="quiz-q"><p>2. The purpose of TRID is to implement Dodd‑Frank ______ ______ ______ rule.</p><div class="quiz-answer"><strong>No Before You Owe.</strong></div></div>
                <div class="quiz-q"><p>3. True or False: LE and CD do not have Regulation Z data.</p><div class="quiz-answer"><strong>False.</strong> They do — APR, finance charge, etc.</div></div>
                <div class="quiz-q"><p>4. First pages of LE and CD have new data such as agent ______ number and ______ ______ confirmation.</p><div class="quiz-answer"><strong>License</strong> and <strong>rate lock</strong>.</div></div>
                <div class="quiz-q"><p>5. LE must be placed in mail within ______ business days of application.</p><div class="quiz-answer"><strong>Three (3).</strong></div></div>
                <div class="quiz-q"><p>6. True or False: There is no waiting period for CD.</p><div class="quiz-answer"><strong>False.</strong> Must receive 3 business days before consummation.</div></div>
                <div class="quiz-q"><p>7. True or False: Settlement agent cannot provide CD to borrower.</p><div class="quiz-answer"><strong>False.</strong> Can if contracted in writing.</div></div>
                <div class="quiz-q"><p>8. Six items that constitute an application?</p><div class="quiz-answer"><strong>Full name, SSN, income, property address, estimated value, desired loan amount.</strong></div></div>
                <div class="quiz-q"><p>9. True or False: Borrower gets appraisal copy only if loan closes.</p><div class="quiz-answer"><strong>False.</strong> Right to copy whether it closes or not.</div></div>
                <div class="quiz-q"><p>10. True or False: Change of circumstance can be due to human error.</p><div class="quiz-answer"><strong>False.</strong> Human error is not valid. Lender absorbs cost.</div></div>
            </section>

            <!-- ==================== COMMON TERMINOLOGY ==================== -->
            <div class="course-header" style="margin-top:1rem;">📕 Common Terminology &amp; Abbreviations</div>

            <section id="terms-aus" class="card">
                <h2>📖 Common Terminology <span class="badge purple">A–Z Reference</span></h2>
                <p>Use the search box below to filter terms quickly. Terms are grouped by category for easy browsing.</p>

                <input type="text" id="termSearch" class="term-search" placeholder="🔍 Search terminology (e.g., LTV, TRID, AUS)..." onkeyup="filterTerms()">

                <!-- AUS & Underwriting -->
                <h3 class="term-category" id="terms-aus">AUS &amp; Underwriting</h3>
                <div class="table-wrap">
                    <table id="termTableAUS">
                        <thead><tr><th style="width:25%;">Term</th><th>Definition</th></tr></thead>
                        <tbody>
                            <tr><td><strong>AUS</strong></td><td>Automated Underwriting System – software that analyzes loan data and provides a credit risk recommendation (e.g., DU, LPA).</td></tr>
                            <tr><td><strong>DU</strong></td><td>Desktop Underwriter – Fannie Mae's automated underwriting system for lenders.</td></tr>
                            <tr><td><strong>DO</strong></td><td>Desktop Originator – Fannie Mae's system for brokers/TPOs to originate and submit loans to sponsoring lenders.</td></tr>
                            <tr><td><strong>LPA</strong></td><td>Loan Product Advisor – Freddie Mac's automated underwriting system.</td></tr>
                            <tr><td><strong>D1C</strong></td><td>Day One Certainty – Fannie Mae's validation service that uses third-party vendor data for VOE, VOI, and VOA.</td></tr>
                            <tr><td><strong>AIM</strong></td><td>Asset and Income Modeler – Freddie Mac's validation service (LPA's equivalent to D1C).</td></tr>
                            <tr><td><strong>VOE</strong></td><td>Verification of Employment – confirmation that a borrower is employed.</td></tr>
                            <tr><td><strong>VOI</strong></td><td>Verification of Income – confirmation of a borrower's income.</td></tr>
                            <tr><td><strong>VOA</strong></td><td>Verification of Assets – confirmation of a borrower's assets.</td></tr>
                            <tr><td><strong>LOS</strong></td><td>Loan Origination System – software used by lenders to manage the loan process (e.g., Encompass, Calyx).</td></tr>
                            <tr><td><strong>TPO</strong></td><td>Third-Party Originator – a broker or correspondent who originates loans for a sponsoring lender.</td></tr>
                            <tr><td><strong>1003</strong></td><td>Uniform Residential Loan Application (URLA) – the standard mortgage application form.</td></tr>
                            <tr><td><strong>1008</strong></td><td>Uniform Underwriting and Transmittal Summary – underwriting transmittal form; DU's analysis report is the equivalent.</td></tr>
                            <tr><td><strong>LLPA</strong></td><td>Loan-Level Price Adjustment – pricing adjustments based on credit score, LTV, occupancy, etc.</td></tr>
                            <tr><td><strong>SFC</strong></td><td>Special Feature Code – codes used when delivering loans to the GSEs (e.g., SFC 127 for DU, SFC 801 for appraisal waiver).</td></tr>
                            <tr><td><strong>DTI</strong></td><td>Debt-to-Income ratio – total monthly debt payments divided by gross monthly income.</td></tr>
                            <tr><td><strong>LTV</strong></td><td>Loan-to-Value ratio – loan amount divided by appraised value or sales price.</td></tr>
                            <tr><td><strong>CLTV</strong></td><td>Combined Loan-to-Value – all liens on the property divided by value.</td></tr>
                            <tr><td><strong>HLTV</strong></td><td>Home Equity Line Total Value – includes total credit line limit (drawn or not) in the CLTV calculation.</td></tr>
                            <tr><td><strong>PITI</strong></td><td>Principal, Interest, Taxes, and Insurance – the total monthly housing payment.</td></tr>
                            <tr><td><strong>MI / PMI</strong></td><td>Mortgage Insurance / Private Mortgage Insurance – required when LTV is above a certain threshold.</td></tr>
                            <tr><td><strong>QM</strong></td><td>Qualified Mortgage – a loan that meets certain ability-to-repay requirements.</td></tr>
                            <tr><td><strong>ATR</strong></td><td>Ability to Repay – Dodd-Frank requirement that lenders ensure borrowers can repay.</td></tr>
